{"name":"Green Black","hex":"#373a3a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green-black","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a color that is a mixture of green and black, resulting in a dark green hue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green-black"}],"entries":[{"title":"Greene Vardiman Black","blocks":[{"text":"Greene Vardiman Black was an American dentist and academic. He was one of the founders of modern dentistry in the United States. He is also known as the father of operative dentistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Greene_Vardiman_Black.jpg/330px-Greene_Vardiman_Black.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greene_Vardiman_Black","wikidata":"Q1356596"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/GV-BLACK.JPG/500px-GV-BLACK.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/GV-BLACK.JPG/500px-GV-BLACK.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"GV Black Classification of Restorations","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGV-BLACK.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/GV_Black.jpg/500px-GV_Black.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/GV_Black.jpg/1280px-GV_Black.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/GV_Black.jpg/500px-GV_Black.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"GV Black Statue in Lincoln Park","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGV_Black.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.